🧠 What is a Data Source in Terraform?
A data source in Terraform is a way to fetch or read existing information from outside Terraform — such as data from AWS, Azure, Vault, GitHub, or even from another Terraform configuration — without creating a new resource.

Think of data sources as "read-only" operations.

💡 Simple Definition:
Data sources let Terraform query existing resources or external data so you can use that information while building or configuring your infrastructure.

⚙️ Syntax of a Data Source
data "<PROVIDER>_<DATA_TYPE>" "<NAME>" {
  # arguments (depends on provider)
}
You can then reference it using:

data.<PROVIDER>_<DATA_TYPE>.<NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE>
🚀 Real-Time Example for DevOps Engineer (AWS)
Let’s say you are creating an EC2 instance in AWS using Terraform,
but you don’t want to hardcode the AMI ID (because AMI IDs change frequently per region).

Instead, you can fetch the latest AMI dynamically using a data source.

Example:
# Get the latest Amazon Linux 2 AMI from AWS
data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" {
  most_recent = true

  filter {
    name   = "name"
    values = ["amzn2-ami-hvm-*-x86_64-gp2"]
  }

  filter {
    name   = "virtualization-type"
    values = ["hvm"]
  }

  owners = ["amazon"]
}

# Use the fetched AMI ID to create an EC2 instance
resource "aws_instance" "web_server" {
  ami           = data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "WebServer"
  }
}
🧩 What’s Happening Here:
The data "aws_ami" "amazon_linux" block queries AWS for the latest Amazon Linux AMI.

Terraform does not create the AMI — it just reads the existing one.

The result (data.aws_ami.amazon_linux.id) is used as input for the EC2 resource.

This ensures your EC2 instance always uses the latest AMI automatically 💥

